01.a|BCDFIJ|Independently encode one empty leaf and one maximum-length key/value page from FORMAT.md, checking every offset,unused byte and CRC. Decode from an intentionally unaligned byte span without casting to a native struct.|Packed layout or host endianness defines the persisted format.|The4096octet image matches explicit fields; unused slots are zero by format; alignment of the source span does not affect byte decoding.|Change format version and define migration versus rejection rather than reinterpret old bytes.|Write a byte-offset table before C structures.|The disk format and in-memory node representation have separate contracts. CRC checks corruption under its model but is not authentication.
01.b|BCDFGJ|Mutate key/value lengths,page identity,count and child reference independently, resealing CRC where necessary so structural checks are exercised. Snapshot caller outputs and ensure references are not followed before validation.|A valid checksum proves all page metadata is safe.|Each structurally invalid but checksummed page rejects before access/publication; exact maximum extents succeed.|Add overlapping logical fields in a future variable-offset format.|Checksum and structural validity are independent predicates.|Resealing deliberately invalid metadata distinguishes semantic validation from checksum-only rejection without targeting any external file format.
01.c|ACDFGJ|Compare fresh format with reopen of established images containing a short page,wrong magic,changed identity and torn checkpoint. Preserve the original failure image and prohibit fallback to an empty tree.|Open failure may safely be repaired by formatting.|Reopen reports failure or the explicitly valid older generation; no established image is silently erased or replaced.|Interrupt initial formatting and specify a separate disposable-image retry protocol.|Fresh creation and recovery are different operations.|The atomic transaction proof does not automatically cover first-time formatting. Recovery must retain evidence needed to diagnose corruption.
02.a|BCFHJ|Trace physical pages colliding in the four-entry clean cache while dirty transaction nodes remain in separate typed scratch. Verify exact tags on hit and derive fixed cache plus scratch memory costs.|A cache slot index identifies the requested page or dirty scratch may be written through.|Only matching physical tags hit; no uncommitted node reaches the clean backend cache; memory categories are distinct.|Use a two-way cache and compare replacement policy without changing transaction ownership.|A hit requires identity equality beyond index equality.|Associativity changes traffic,not the no-steal invariant. Sharing a buffer between clean cache and dirty scratch would require a new ownership proof.
02.b|BCDFGJ|Prime a home-page cache entry,commit a changed value,read through the WAL view,checkpoint and read again. Remove each invalidation separately and retain the smallest stale-generation counterexample.|One invalidation at commit covers every later physical-view transition.|Both publication and checkpoint transitions preserve the latest committed logical map; each missing-invalidation mutant is distinguishable.|Reuse a physical page after deletion and add generation to the cache identity analysis.|Logical page identity can map to different physical authoritative bytes.|Cache coherence is part of storage correctness. A correct tree lookup over stale bytes still returns the wrong committed value.
02.c|BCFHIJ|Count hits and backend reads on repeated and conflict traces with a known cold cache. Report page bytes and fixed memory separately from actual filesystem/device traffic.|A warm cache hit is measured faster storage hardware.|The trace predicts exact logical read counts; native device traffic remains a separately observed or unavailable quantity.|Run a scan larger than the cache and compare with a point-lookup workload.|Choose the layer at which each counter increments.|A direct-mapped conflict workload can defeat apparent locality. Finite traffic counts do not establish latency or physical I/O size.
03.a|ABCFJ|Derive unsigned length-delimited ordering for empty-invalid keys,embedded zero,prefix pairs and high-bit octets. At every separator equality show why descent selects the right child.|strcmp or signed char order implements the key contract.|Shorter equal prefixes sort first; embedded zeros are data; equality follows the right-minimum convention.|Change collation to case-folded text under a new key-normalization contract.|The separator rule depends on the chosen ordering relation.|A new collation may merge formerly distinct keys and invalidate persisted separators. It is a format/semantic migration,not a comparator swap alone.
03.b|BCDFGJ|Test lower-bound scans starting before,at,between and after keys with callbacks stopping after zero,one or several accepted records. Verify NOT_FOUND/FULL leave get outputs unchanged and define callback borrow lifetime.|Early stop is an error or a callback can retain a transient page pointer indefinitely.|Ordered prefixes match the independent map; normal early stop is distinct from corruption; copied ownership is required for retained values.|Permit callback reentry only after deriving a new serialization/lifetime contract.|A callback view's lifetime is part of scan semantics.|The current exclusive-caller design does not imply reentrant safety. Reentry can invalidate scratch/cache state and must not be assumed.
03.c|BCDFJ|Cross-check tree traversal,leaf chain and allocation bitmap after each update. Inject a dangling child,a leaf cycle and an allocated unreachable page; require bounded rejection before following more than the page limit.|Successful lookup of one key proves the whole tree representation.|All three views agree on reachable nodes and ordered records; corrupt cycles terminate with rejection,not an unbounded walk.|Add a shared subtree and explain why a tree ownership invariant rejects it even without a cycle.|Track visited identity as well as recursion depth.|A depth bound alone may miss duplicate ownership. The representation requires each allocated reachable node exactly once.
04.a|ABCFJ|Hand-derive a seven-key leaf overflow into private eight-entry scratch,then a cascading internal split and root growth. Check copied versus promoted separator behavior,child intervals and leaf links after each step.|Leaf and internal splits move the same separator in the same way.|Leaf split retains all records and copies the right minimum upward; internal promotion partitions child intervals without losing a key range.|Use descending and alternating-end insertion to change split direction while preserving invariants.|Separate records stored in leaves from separator copies.|A visually balanced shape is insufficient; separator minima and child ownership determine search correctness.
04.b|BCDFGJ|Inject page-allocation failure at every split-propagation allocation. Compare replacement of an existing key with insertion of a new key and preserve the parent's whole-transaction abort policy on capacity exhaustion.|A failed insert may leave earlier staged work committed or replacement always needs a new page.|Committed map survives capacity failure; the transaction state follows the specified abort policy; invalid input has its separately documented preservation behavior.|Design operation-level rollback instead of transaction abort and enumerate required undo state.|Different failure classes have different transaction consequences.|Stronger rollback requires staging or undo for every modified node and allocation. It cannot be claimed from unchanged final root alone.
04.c|BCDFHJ|Kill a wrong-separator propagation mutant using a minimized insertion sequence and compare every key/value with an independent ordered map. Instrument full separator refresh and report its O(P) cost.|A B+tree implementation is automatically logarithmic per update regardless of maintenance strategy.|Whole-map equality and structural checks hold; measured node visits expose the retained full-refresh cost.|Replace full refresh with ancestor-path updates and prove the exact affected minima.|Count maintenance work as well as search descent.|An optimization is acceptable only under the same invariants and failure contract. Faster lookup on a few keys does not validate separator repair.
05.a|ABCFJ|Derive left borrow,right borrow,merge and root contraction from exact leaf/internal minima. Include deleting a subtree minimum without underflow and update the ancestor separator.|Only underflow can require separator repair.|Every resulting node satisfies occupancy and child intervals; a changed minimum propagates even when occupancy remains valid.|Change node capacities and derive new minima/split rules before reusing tests.|Minimum-key changes and occupancy changes are separate triggers.|The parent7-key policy is part of the representation. Substituting constants without rederiving balancing rules can break invariants.
05.b|BCDFGJ|Track allocation bitmap and all tree/leaf references when merging and reusing a page. Reject reuse until no authoritative reference names the freed page; inject a stale leaf-link mutant.|Clearing the allocation bit alone makes a page reusable.|No reachable free page or unreachable allocated node remains; scans never follow the retired identity into a new node.|Add generation-tagged page references under a versioned format.|Freeing is an ownership transition across every reference structure.|A generation detects stale references only if stored and validated everywhere. It does not repair missing link updates by itself.
05.c|BCDFHJ|Run the parent's92-key deletion permutation with whole-map checks after every step,then derive a separate adversarial trace forcing both redistribution directions and multilevel merge. Reinsert to expose leaked page capacity.|One final empty tree proves every intermediate update and reuse was correct.|Each prefix matches the independent map and structural checker; reinsertion reuses released capacity rather than exhausting early.|Mix replacement,missing deletion and new insertion under a fixed seed.|A prefix oracle catches transient representation errors.|A permutation visits all original keys because37 and92 are coprime; that arithmetic fact does not guarantee it exercises every balancing branch.
06.a|ABCFHJ|Derive memory ownership for clean cache,committed WAL view,transaction scratch,I/O buffers and call stack. Enforce one serialized caller including readers and prove dirty private nodes cannot escape to home pages.|One writer permits concurrent readers automatically or bounded cache means bounded total memory.|The complete memory bound includes every category; no reader concurrency is implied; backend writes before commit are restricted by protocol.|Design snapshot readers and list the additional version/reclamation machinery.|No-steal is a publication invariant,not a locking implementation.|Adding readers changes visibility and lifetime rules. The existing serialized proof cannot be reused as a concurrency proof.
06.b|BCDFGJ|Exercise nested begin,commit without begin,explicit abort,input rejection and capacity-induced abort. Compare committed map and staged earlier updates after each result according to the exact parent policy.|Every error has the same rollback boundary.|Input rejection preserves staged earlier work; capacity exhaustion aborts the transaction; committed state remains unchanged before publication.|Introduce a savepoint and specify its bounded undo representation.|Write the state machine before error handling.|Savepoints require a finer rollback boundary and resource accounting. A single active flag does not encode the required history.
06.c|ADFGJ|Diagnose a deliberately leaked uncommitted update by tracing whether bytes came from private scratch,clean cache,home or committed WAL. Use equal logical page IDs with distinct authoritative generations.|A correct value lookup proves it came from committed state.|The first unauthorized visibility transition is identified and repaired; committed reads cannot observe transaction-private bytes.|Add a cache shared by two store handles and reassess serialization assumptions.|Record provenance of each read,not only returned data.|Shared caches introduce identity and synchronization obligations. A handle-local invalidation proof does not cover another handle's cached state.
07.a|ABCFGJ|Enumerate persistence cuts around old-marker invalidation/flush,all after-image writes/flush and new-marker write/flush. Derive why each surviving authoritative marker implies complete required data under the declared model.|Write order in source implies persistence order without barriers.|No reused redo bytes coexist with an authoritative old marker; a new valid marker follows durable after-images; acknowledged success recovers new state.|Allow a flush to lie and identify exactly which implication fails.|State each durable fact established by a successful barrier.|The proof is conditional on the adapter. A failed premise invalidates the guarantee rather than becoming another admitted old-or-new schedule.
07.b|ACDFGJ|Inject errors before publication,during marker publication and after complete persistence but before acknowledgment. Verify definite abort versus INDETERMINATE and poisoned-handle behavior; require reopen/reconciliation before further work.|An error return proves the transaction did not commit.|Prepublication failures recover old state under the model; publication uncertainty may recover old or new; poisoned handles refuse ordinary use.|Expose the operation through a non-idempotent application service and add an operation identifier.|The response boundary follows the persistence decision.|An application-level retry needs its own deduplication/reconciliation contract. The storage error category must not be flattened into a Boolean failure.
07.c|BCDFGJ|Compile the omitted-commit-flush mutant and find a cut where it acknowledges success but recovery returns old state. Contrast with a lost acknowledgment that leaves the complete new map durable.|Any old-or-new result is acceptable even after reported success.|Acknowledged success requires new state; the mutant violates that stronger relation,while indeterminate response permits either complete map.|Delay the flush acknowledgment after persistence and test the caller's recovery path.|The oracle depends on both return status and recovered map.|Crash consistency is not merely open-success or structural validity. Application semantics and acknowledgment conditions are essential.
08.a|BCDFGJ|Corrupt the last committed after-image while earlier images remain valid. Verify complete batch validation occurs before any home write; then test interrupted full-page installation with intact WAL evidence.|Validating each page immediately before copying it preserves failure atomicity for a corrupt later page.|Corrupt committed input causes zero home writes; valid interrupted replay remains recoverable under the retained marker.|Use a streaming validator with bounded scratch and prove how it still validates the full batch first.|Validation order and installation order are separate phases.|Streaming memory optimization cannot weaken the all-before-write validation contract. A two-pass approach trades reads for bounded storage.
08.b|ABCFGJ|Trace both checkpoint slots through torn replacement,highest-valid selection,generation exhaustion and WAL reclamation. Keep old metadata plus newer valid WAL as a recoverable state.|The largest numeric generation is usable even with invalid checksum or generations may wrap safely.|Only valid metadata participates; generation wrap rejects; WAL remains until new checkpoint discoverability is durable.|Add a third metadata slot and derive selection under the same fault model.|Validity precedes ordering.|More copies do not automatically create a correct selection protocol. Generation and durability relationships must remain unambiguous.
08.c|BCDFGJ|Kill early-WAL-reclamation by interrupting checkpoint after some homes change but before new metadata is durable. Demonstrate loss of the only complete explanation and repair the order.|Checkpoint is a second commit or clearing the log early is harmless housekeeping.|The faulty schedule cannot justify the mixed home state; corrected order preserves the acknowledged new map at every admitted cut.|Interrupt the repaired recovery itself and repeat reopen.|Identify which surviving record authorizes reconstruction.|Checkpoint transfers authority from log to home plus metadata. Reclamation is safe only after that authority transfer is durable.
09.a|BCDFGJ|Reopen images with old checkpoint/new marker,wrong generation,count mismatch,invalid after-image and short read. Validate all required bytes before replay and preserve the original failed image.|Any recognizable marker authorizes recovery or corruption may become an empty database.|Only a complete generation-consistent batch replays; failure reports retain image evidence and no fresh format occurs.|Add a stale valid marker older than the selected checkpoint and justify its treatment.|Compare marker generation with accepted checkpoint authority.|Recovery is interpretation of durable evidence. A plausible page alone does not establish a complete transaction history.
09.b|ABCFGJ|Cut recovery at each home write and flush,then restart recovery again. Prove full-page redo idempotence at the byte and application-map levels while retaining the marker until installation is durable.|Recovery cannot fail or replaying a transaction twice always doubles its effects.|Repeated full-page replacement yields the same recovered map; logical increment replay would require a different idempotence mechanism.|Replace after-images with logical operations and design sequence/deduplication metadata.|Distinguish replacement data from commands.|Idempotence is a property of the logged operation and protocol. It is not supplied automatically by calling the process recovery.
09.c|ACDFGJ|Separate admitted torn unsynchronized writes from corruption after acknowledged persistence. Inject each class and require the corresponding old/new recovery or corruption rejection,without broadening the fault model after failure.|Every damaged image is allowed to return old state under crash consistency.|Post-acknowledgment corruption is not silently accepted as an old transaction; failure images remain preserved and unsupported faults are explicit.|Construct a CRC collision analysis and explain why checksum validity cannot authenticate data.|Write the fault assumptions beside the oracle.|The parent excludes checksum collisions and lying flushes. Demonstrating those limits is valuable but does not falsify a theorem that explicitly excludes them.
10.a|BCDFGJ|Audit stable/pending state in the simulated medium with tiny page histories: no persistence,partial persistence,reordered pending writes,tear and error after persistence. Give each schedule a replayable identity.|A simulated write failure means no bytes changed or a model is a real power cut.|The model preserves its declared admitted transitions and records which bytes became stable; it is labeled simulated evidence.|Enumerate all pending subsets for a reduced two-page toy protocol.|A simulator needs tests independent of the storage engine.|A faulty medium can make an incorrect protocol appear safe. Reduced exhaustive schedules help verify the simulator's semantics without claiming all physical behavior.
10.b|BCDFGJ|Compare the entire recovered key/value map against an independent transaction oracle across commit,checkpoint and repeated-recovery cuts. Include deletion and replacement so partial mixtures cannot pass a single-key check.|Successful reopen or one correct key proves transaction atomicity.|Only complete old/new maps are allowed where specified; acknowledged success and checkpoint recovery require the complete new map.|Add two successive transactions with overlapping keys and distinguish their allowed durable prefixes.|The oracle must know the application transaction boundary.|Checking each key independently for old-or-new permits impossible mixed transactions. Whole-map consistency is the relevant property.
10.c|DFGHJ|Publish exact selected cuts,persistence modes,mutants and uncovered schedules. Minimize a failing cut to its first invalid persistence transition and derive an additional schedule class beyond the five representative modes.|A finite campaign exhausts all tear offsets,pending subsets and devices.|Counts describe actually executed cases; the order proof and finite observations remain separate; new schedules are justified by a mechanism.|Use a bounded model checker for a reduced protocol and state the abstraction relation to CST2.|Coverage of event boundaries is not coverage of every persisted subset.|Model checking can exhaust a finite abstraction while leaving the abstraction's fidelity and physical adapter assumptions open.
11.a|BCDFGJ|Inject every positive short transfer,EINTR,zero and terminal error into one4096-byte page operation. Check offset arithmetic and exact bytes; distinguish partial read failure from a complete page.|A single pread/pwrite always transfers a page or restarting repeats no data.|Only positive progress advances; zero/error terminates under the contract; no incomplete page is decoded as complete.|Change to a nonblocking backend with explicit readiness and retry budget.|The page contract is implemented over a byte-progress loop.|A new backend can introduce EAGAIN and asynchronous lifetime. Those require a different adapter contract,not an unbounded retry.
11.b|BCDFGIJ|Commit,close and reopen a disposable preallocated image through the actual file adapter,recording fsync status and platform identity. Inject a failed sync separately and preserve uncertainty.|Readback after close proves survival of physical power loss or file-content sync covers new pathname durability.|The test establishes the requested API/reopen boundary only; sync failure is reported and no physical power-cut result is inferred.|Create the file during the operation and add the directory-entry durability obligation.|Name which object the synchronization covers.|Preallocation intentionally removes creation semantics from the original contract. Extending the lifetime boundary adds metadata persistence work.
11.c|ABFHIJ|Calculate exact logical page reads,writes and flushes for a small value update,then compare with accepted bytes and useful changed payload. Keep filesystem/device traffic and elapsed time separate.|A page-write count equals physical device I/O or amplification predicts speed directly.|Full-image redo costs are explicit; ratio denominator is defined and nonzero; cache/device observations retain scope.|Implement changed-page redo under a new protocol and compare correctness-equivalent amplification.|Optimization must preserve commit and recovery semantics.|Logging fewer pages changes batch description,validation and recovery completeness. A lower traffic count alone does not validate the new protocol.
12.a|BCDFGJ|Bind narrow VM services to the fixed-key integer store adapter with checked byte encoding and distinct host-budget,capacity and commit outcomes. Reject unknown service IDs without exposing native paths/pointers.|A VM host error proves storage rollback or a narrow signature enforces every capability policy.|Each layer's status remains attributable; denied services have no side effects; uncertain commit requires recovery.|Add a read-modify-write service with operation IDs and reconciliation.|List the side effect boundary of each service.|An integer API can still perform persistent effects. Capability restriction and idempotence are separate contracts.
12.b|BCDFGIJ|Adapt the original kernel whole-page device API to an exclusive image slice. Check user-buffer permissions,checked slice bounds,complete transfer and flush semantics before claiming CST2 compatibility.|A block read/write API automatically satisfies the store's durability model.|The adapter preserves page identity and exclusive ownership; short/error/device-completion behavior is explicit; actual guest execution is recorded separately.|Replace the device with asynchronous queued I/O and derive buffer retention/cancellation rules.|Translate every store_io promise into a backend obligation.|A common C signature does not imply equivalent semantics. The adapter is part of the proof and requires its own failure tests.
12.c|BCDFGJ|Execute write,lost-response failure,restart and read through the same application boundary. Compare prepublication abort with postpublication uncertainty and reconcile before any non-idempotent retry.|An application exception or timeout proves absence of durable effects.|Recovered value determines the outcome under the operation contract; no mixed map or blind retry is accepted.|Transfer the protocol to a two-step application workflow with compensation.|Keep application response and storage commitment separate.|Compensation is another operation that can fail; it is not automatic rollback of an already durable transaction.
